1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,816
In 1949, Joseph Campbell gave the hero a thousand faces.

2
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:10,311
Gilgamesh, the Buddha, King Arthur.

3
00:00:10,311 --> 00:00:13,700
He believed they were one figure, wearing different masks.

4
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:15,923
But what if a thousand was too few?

5
00:00:16,373 --> 00:00:18,523
What if the hero has infinite faces?

6
00:00:20,023 --> 00:00:22,097
Campbell called it the monomyth.

7
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:25,532
The hero leaves home, is tested, and returns changed.

8
00:00:25,982 --> 00:00:28,787
He drew it as a circle of seventeen stages:

9
00:00:28,787 --> 00:00:32,701
the call, the mentor, the threshold, the ordeal, the return.

10
00:00:33,151 --> 00:00:36,847
It is a beautiful map. But the territory keeps refusing it.

11
00:00:37,297 --> 00:00:42,962
Folklorists pointed out that he chose the stories that fit, and quietly left out the ones that didn't.

12
00:00:43,412 --> 00:00:47,802
Real heroes skip stages, repeat them, fail them, and invent new ones.

13
00:00:48,252 --> 00:00:52,122
The wise mentor, the magic sword, the guardian at the gate.

14
00:00:52,122 --> 00:00:53,697
These are local details.

15
00:00:53,697 --> 00:00:54,287
Costumes.

16
00:00:54,737 --> 00:00:57,424
Underneath the costumes, something else repeats.

17
00:00:58,924 --> 00:00:59,757
Look at Link.

18
00:01:00,207 --> 00:01:07,837
In Ocarina of Time he is a boy from the forest, the only child without a fairy, until the Great Deku Tree calls him.

19
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:13,788
He draws the Master Sword, and wakes seven years later in a Hyrule fallen to Ganondorf.

20
00:01:14,238 --> 00:01:19,450
Every dungeon is a problem. Every death is an error. Every retry is a correction.

21
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:24,129
And when the story ends, Link does not stay. He returns as someone else.

22
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:26,717
A boy who sails a drowned world.

23
00:01:26,717 --> 00:01:29,323
A hero forced into the shape of a wolf.

24
00:01:29,323 --> 00:01:32,062
A knight who wakes after a hundred years.

25
00:01:32,512 --> 00:01:35,069
A new body, a new Hyrule, a new problem.

26
00:01:35,519 --> 00:01:39,650
Across the series, Link is a microcosm of the whole idea:

27
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:44,869
one hero, reborn again and again into problems no one has solved before.

28
00:01:46,369 --> 00:01:49,213
The oldest hero we know made the oldest mistake.

29
00:01:49,663 --> 00:01:54,621
When his friend Enkidu died, Gilgamesh decided the problem was death itself.

30
00:01:55,071 --> 00:02:00,082
He crossed the waters of death to find Utnapishtim, the man who survived the flood.

31
00:02:00,532 --> 00:02:04,715
His test was to stay awake for seven nights. He fell asleep at once.

32
00:02:05,165 --> 00:02:09,496
He was given a plant that restores youth. A snake stole it while he bathed.

33
00:02:09,946 --> 00:02:14,583
So he went home, and showed the boatman the walls of Uruk, the city he had built.

34
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:17,861
He never solved death. He corrected the question.

35
00:02:19,361 --> 00:02:24,770
Thousands of years later, a soldier in green armour stands on a ring the size of a world.

36
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:32,584
Master Chief is told that the way to stop the Flood, a parasite that consumes every living mind, is to fire Halo.

37
00:02:33,034 --> 00:02:36,618
It is the obvious solution. It is also a catastrophe.

38
00:02:37,068 --> 00:02:42,107
Halo does not kill the Flood. It kills their food: every thinking being in range.

39
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:47,075
The guide who promised salvation was asking him to silence the galaxy.

40
00:02:47,525 --> 00:02:49,331
So he does the harder thing.

41
00:02:49,331 --> 00:02:54,877
He turns back, and with Cortana, overloads his own ship's engines to destroy the ring.

42
00:02:55,327 --> 00:02:59,794
Sometimes the hero's greatest act is refusing the answer he was handed.

43
00:03:01,294 --> 00:03:04,758
In Berserk, Guts loses everything in a single night:

44
00:03:04,758 --> 00:03:08,955
his comrades, his arm, his eye, and the friend he trusted most.

45
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:14,911
His world is ruled by causality, a fate that seems to have planned every horror in advance.

46
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:17,834
For two years he answers with revenge.

47
00:03:17,834 --> 00:03:21,870
He hunts demons, and leaves Casca, the woman he loves, behind.

48
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:26,338
It is an error, and the old blacksmith who sheltered them tells him so.

49
00:03:26,788 --> 00:03:30,312
Guts turns back. Revenge slowly gives way to protection.

50
00:03:30,762 --> 00:03:34,181
The story calls those who resist fate strugglers.

51
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:36,414
Guts has no destiny on his side.

52
00:03:36,414 --> 00:03:38,159
Only the refusal to stop.

53
00:03:39,659 --> 00:03:41,281
Aragorn inherits a failure.

54
00:03:41,731 --> 00:03:47,153
His ancestor Isildur stood at the fire of Mount Doom with the Ring in his hand, and kept it.

55
00:03:47,603 --> 00:03:52,435
As a child, Aragorn was hidden under another name: Estel, which means hope.

56
00:03:52,885 --> 00:03:57,678
He spends decades as a wanderer before he accepts the crown he was born to.

57
00:03:58,128 --> 00:04:01,530
And when the last battle comes, he does not march for victory.

58
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:08,030
He leads a small army to the Black Gate of Mordor, to draw the Dark Lord's eye away from two hobbits.

59
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:11,636
His plan is not to win. It is to buy them a chance.

60
00:04:13,136 --> 00:04:15,693
Those two hobbits break Campbell's map completely.

61
00:04:16,143 --> 00:04:20,337
At the edge of the fire, Frodo fails. He claims the Ring for himself.

62
00:04:20,787 --> 00:04:24,400
The quest survives because of mercy shown long before.

63
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,942
Bilbo spared Gollum, and so did Frodo.

64
00:04:27,392 --> 00:04:31,703
And it is Gollum, of all creatures, who falls into the fire with the Ring.

65
00:04:32,153 --> 00:04:35,252
When Sam cannot carry the Ring for him, he carries Frodo.

66
00:04:35,702 --> 00:04:40,627
Frodo does go home. But he cannot stay. Something in him was never repaired.

67
00:04:41,077 --> 00:04:46,691
No clean return. No single hero. Errors, mercy, and hope, passed from hand to hand.

68
00:04:48,191 --> 00:04:51,173
The figures they meet are not stock characters either.

69
00:04:51,623 --> 00:04:57,592
Gandalf, the Great Deku Tree, the elders and the old books are the wisdom of traditions:

70
00:04:57,592 --> 00:05:00,576
answers earlier problem-solvers left behind.

71
00:05:01,026 --> 00:05:04,261
Wisdom is not a destination. It is a head start.

72
00:05:04,711 --> 00:05:09,219
And the villains, Ganondorf, Vader, Ravana, are the problem given a face.

73
00:05:10,719 --> 00:05:15,245
Nausicaä lives beside a toxic forest that is slowly swallowing her world.

74
00:05:15,695 --> 00:05:17,289
Everyone else wants to burn it.

75
00:05:17,739 --> 00:05:19,208
She studies it instead.

76
00:05:19,208 --> 00:05:25,084
In a secret room, she grows its plants in clean water and clean sand, and they are harmless.

77
00:05:25,534 --> 00:05:29,009
The forest was never the problem. The poisoned soil was.

78
00:05:29,459 --> 00:05:31,548
Her heroism is an explanation.

79
00:05:33,048 --> 00:05:38,534
In Blade Runner 2049, the replicant K comes to believe he is a miracle:

80
00:05:38,534 --> 00:05:41,238
a child born to a replicant mother.

81
00:05:41,688 --> 00:05:43,469
For a while, he has a destiny.

82
00:05:43,919 --> 00:05:48,900
Then he learns he was wrong. He is not the chosen one. He is no one in particular.

83
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:51,365
And he does the heroic thing anyway.

84
00:05:51,365 --> 00:05:54,892
He saves Deckard, and brings him to the daughter he never knew.

85
00:05:55,342 --> 00:05:59,120
A hero without a prophecy. A choice, made after the error.

86
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:03,389
So what actually repeats beneath all these faces?

87
00:06:03,839 --> 00:06:05,695
Not the stages. The cycle.

88
00:06:06,145 --> 00:06:06,824
A problem.

89
00:06:06,824 --> 00:06:07,367
A guess.

90
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:07,978
An error.

91
00:06:07,978 --> 00:06:08,861
A correction.

92
00:06:08,861 --> 00:06:12,391
And then a new problem that no one has faced before.

93
00:06:12,841 --> 00:06:18,980
Karl Popper argued that all life is problem solving, and that knowledge grows by finding our mistakes.

94
00:06:19,430 --> 00:06:21,252
David Deutsch went further.

95
00:06:21,252 --> 00:06:24,627
Every person, he argues, is a universal explainer:

96
00:06:24,627 --> 00:06:29,082
able, in principle, to understand anything that can be understood.

97
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:32,523
Problems are inevitable. Problems are soluble.

98
00:06:32,973 --> 00:06:37,710
He called his book The Beginning of Infinity, because that process need never end.

99
00:06:39,210 --> 00:06:41,922
That is why the hero never repeats exactly.

100
00:06:42,372 --> 00:06:46,141
Every problem situation is new, so every hero must be new.

101
00:06:46,591 --> 00:06:48,735
A farm boy on a desert planet.

102
00:06:48,735 --> 00:06:51,022
A hacker who wakes from a dream.

103
00:06:51,022 --> 00:06:53,380
A fighter who passes every limit.

104
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:55,524
An outcast who keeps his word.

105
00:06:55,524 --> 00:06:57,525
A cyborg asking what she is.

106
00:06:57,975 --> 00:07:00,873
The variety is not noise. It is the evidence.

107
00:07:01,323 --> 00:07:05,060
If the hero were a formula, a thousand faces might be enough.

108
00:07:05,510 --> 00:07:09,207
But the hero is a problem-solver, and problems never run out.

109
00:07:09,657 --> 00:07:13,869
Campbell saw the faces. Popper and Deutsch show us what lies behind them.

110
00:07:14,319 --> 00:07:16,551
Call it the Hero With Infinite Faces.

111
00:07:17,001 --> 00:07:18,982
And one of those faces could be yours.

112
00:07:19,432 --> 00:07:21,800
A hero is not someone who never fails.

113
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,725
It is someone who never stops correcting, and never loses hope.
